Section: EMPLOYEE HANDBOOK > Rights, Obligations and Conduct

Rights, Obligations and Conduct Every employee has the right to
• be provided with the role described in the Employment Agreement;
• an equipped workplace and the materials needed to perform the job;
• timely payment of salary, in full;
• the leave entitlement set out in the Leave Policy; and
• a lunch break during the working day. Every employee is obligated to
• conduct themselves in a way that strengthens the image and reputation of The Company;
• fulfil their responsibilities in good faith, with loyalty, accuracy and attentiveness;
• understand and follow every internal policy, protocol and SOP adopted by The Company;

Section: EMPLOYEE HANDBOOK > Rights, Obligations and Conduct

• understand and follow every internal policy, protocol and SOP adopted by The Company;
• comply with hygiene and dress standards. Client-facing employees must wear smart casuals or office formals when representing The Company. Employees are expected to maintain a well-groomed appearance. Reasonable accommodation will be made for requirements arising from religion, disability or a medical condition; please inform HR, who will handle such requests respectfully and confidentially.
• contribute ideas on equipment, technology and work systems that aid The Company’s development;

Section: EMPLOYEE HANDBOOK > Rights, Obligations and Conduct

• contribute ideas on equipment, technology and work systems that aid The Company’s development;
• comply with rules on the storage of files, materials and equipment, and help prevent misuse or theft of Company property;
• use raw materials, electricity, fuel and other resources economically;
• comply with non-disclosure and data-confidentiality requirements relating to the data, systems and property of The Company;
• immediately report the loss of any Company-issued ID card or other aid that could be misused by third parties. Acts that may attract disciplinary action The following are treated as misconduct and may result in disciplinary action under the Policy on Disciplinary Action:

Section: EMPLOYEE HANDBOOK > Performance Improvement Plan (PIP) Policy

“Sexual harassment” includes any one or more of the following unwelcome acts or behaviour, whether direct or by implication:
• physical contact or advances;
• a demand or request for sexual favours;
• sexually coloured remarks, or pranks, teasing or jokes of a sexual nature;
• showing pornography;
• any other unwelcome physical, verbal or non-verbal conduct of a sexual nature;
• an implied or explicit promise of preferential treatment in employment;
• an implied or explicit threat of detrimental treatment in employment;
• a threat about present or future employment;
• interference with work, or creating an intimidating, offensive or hostile work environment; or

Section: EMPLOYEE HANDBOOK > Performance Improvement Plan (PIP) Policy

• interference with work, or creating an intimidating, offensive or hostile work environment; or
• humiliating treatment likely to affect health or safety. Sexual harassment may occur between any persons connected with the workplace, including co-workers, and between an employee and any person they deal with in the course of work, whether or not that person is an employee of The Company. What constitutes a workplace For the purposes of this Policy, a “workplace” includes:
• The Company’s office located in Bhavnagar, and any other location to which The Company may expand from time to time;
• any place visited by an employee arising out of or during the course of employment;

