• transportation provided or arranged by The Company for commuting or business purposes;
• any social gathering organised by The Company, including business meetings and parties; and

Section: EMPLOYEE HANDBOOK > Policy on Disciplinary Action

Policy on Disciplinary Action This policy sets out how The Company handles disciplinary issues - allegations or complaints about an employee, breaches of this Handbook or Company policy, and other matters that in Management’s opinion require disciplinary action. It applies to all employees and is applied consistently and fairly. Conduct not specifically described in this policy may still be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including dismissal. Procedural Fairness Where an allegation may lead to a formal disciplinary process, The Company will ensure that:
• the employee is informed of their right to be accompanied by a representative or support person at any stage;

Section: EMPLOYEE HANDBOOK > Policy on Disciplinary Action

• the employee is informed of their right to be accompanied by a representative or support person at any stage;
• the employee receives notice of the specific allegation, its seriousness and the possible consequences if substantiated;
• the employee has the opportunity to provide an explanation, which is given full consideration; and
• matters discussed in a disciplinary process are kept confidential, with a record kept on the personnel file. Procedure

Section: EMPLOYEE HANDBOOK > Policy on Disciplinary Action

• Notes of the meeting are taken by a management representative (these are not verbatim). All responses and evidence are considered by the committee.
• Outcome: The outcome is delivered at a separate meeting after full consideration, with a further opportunity for the employee to be heard before the final decision is communicated. The outcome is confirmed in writing. Possible Outcomes If the outcome is against the employee, the disciplinary committee may impose one or more of the following, proportionate to the misconduct:
• a written warning or written apology;
• referral to professional help or support, where relevant;

Section: EMPLOYEE HANDBOOK > Policy on Disciplinary Action

• a written warning or written apology;
• referral to professional help or support, where relevant;
• suspension (with the question of pay during suspension determined in accordance with applicable law);
• a fine or deduction, only on the grounds, within the limits and following the procedure permitted by the Code on Wages, 2019; or
